{"text": "In many situations. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Corporate moral decisions do not entail a trade off. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Doing the right thing. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Is sometimes good for business. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Legal scholar Lucian Bebchuk calls this type of reasoning. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Instrumental stakeholderism. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Making stakeholders customers, the community, employees, and so on happy. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Even if it costs a little. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> May be in the interest of shareholders. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> For Bebchuk. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Such situations do not pose a challenge. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> To economist Milton Friedmans view. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> That managers should aim at maximizing profits. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Giving an extra week of paid vacation or long maternity leave. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> May help attract. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> The most talented young college graduates. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Sourcing inputs responsibly. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> May appeal to millennial consumers who are concerned with the environment. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Being more environmentally conscious. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Than what current standards require. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Might give a leading edge. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> If one anticipates. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> That regulations will become tighter in the near term. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Morality can be seen in such cases. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> As an instrument for current and long term profits. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Not necessarily as a goal for its own sake.", "alignment": {"characters": ["I", "n", " ", "m", "a", "n", "y", " ", "s", "i", "t", "u", "a", "t", "i", "o", "n", "s", ".", " ", "<", "b", "r", "e", "a", "k", " ", "t", "i", "m", "e", "=", "\"", "0", ".", "8", "s", "\"", " ", "/", ">", " ", "C", "o", "r", "p", "o", "r", "a", "t", "e", " ", "m", "o", "r", "a", "l", " ", "d", "e", "c", "i", "s", "i", "o", 